      "explanation": "Each path has two records because the two boxes' f16 sources differ: the Mac's qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct-f16.gguf is a26884ee... and the dgxspark's is aef12ad4..., a minfer producer-version difference. Both records are now -ffp-contract=fast builds (dgxspark GCC 13.3.0; Mac Apple clang 21.0.0 build-fpc); before 2026-10-07 the Mac record was an Apple clang build *without* the flag, so the same source yielded a third content. Rebuilding the Mac binary with the flag (issue #342) made the five legacy quads match minfer 290/290 with the pass line `build -ffp-contract=fast`, but the six K-quant records (q4_K/q5_K/q6_K on qwen2.5-0.5b and qwen3-0.6b) still match neither minfer's Fast nor its uncontracted model (`tensor ... payload differs ... build unmatched`): the K-quant encoder source is byte-identical between the two builds' revisions (050dde50c..c479922ac changed only qkx3/i-quant code), so that residual is an Apple-clang-vs-GCC codegen difference, not a -ffp-contract one. The gate names a non-contracting build when it sees one (issue #334, PR #340). Since issue #349 the record also carries the decision this divergence forces: the dgxspark gcc 13.3.0 entry is the `authoritative_reference` for each of these paths — the build the byte-parity claim was measured against — and a reference whose digest matches the Mac entry (a recorded content, so the file itself is honest) is now named and skipped loudly rather than reported as `build unmatched`: parity for these targets is asserted only against the recorded compiler. A mismatch against the authoritative entry, or against a file no entry records, still fails.",
      "references": [
        "docs/GGUF-TOOLING.md 4.2",
        "https://github.com/yusiwen/minfer/issues/342"
      ]
    }
